# issue-graph configuration.
#
# Workspaces — including your Linear API key — are NOT configured here any
# more. Start the app and it will show a setup form; everything you add there
# is stored in data/workspaces.db.
#
# What remains below is the handful of settings the server needs before it can
# open a database, so they cannot live in one. Every value has a working
# default: an empty .env is a valid .env, and docker-compose.yml already pins
# PORT and SQLITE_PATH.

# Where the data lives. The workspace roster is workspaces.db beside this path;
# each workspace's cache is workspaces/<id>/graph.db under the same directory.
# SQLITE_PATH=./data/graph.db

# Port the server listens on.
# PORT=31415

# Shown in Settings so you can tell instances apart (laptop vs. homelab).
# INSTANCE_LABEL=personal-local

# trace | debug | info | warn | error
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Snapshot schedule. These stay here rather than in Settings because they are
# read on paths that run before a workspace is resolved. The hour is LOCAL
# time, so under Docker set TZ as well or it will fire on UTC.
# DAILY_SNAPSHOT_HOUR=2
# SNAPSHOT_RETENTION_DAYS=365

# Design-doc progress. Point at a repo containing openspec/changes/*/ or a
# .spectra.yaml. Must be absolute. Leave unset to disable the feature — which
# is the right choice on a remote server, where the repo is not checked out.
# REPO_PATH=/absolute/path/to/your/repo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT configurable here
#
# LINEAR_API_ENDPOINT is fixed at https://api.linear.app/graphql. It is not
# exposed in the setup UI either: anyone who could change it could point the
# app at their own host and receive your API key in the Authorization header
# on the next sync. If you genuinely need a proxy, edit src/backend/lib/env.ts
# and rebuild, so the change is a deployment decision rather than a form field.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Advanced: custom label schema — see label-schema.example.yaml.
